I work with a city planner who's trying to figure out which public lockers to put around town and which neighborhood of commuters should use each installed locker. The idea is simple: every locker that gets installed costs money, and every commuter who has to detour to reach their assigned locker pays a little extra in time or distance. The planner needs to pick which lockers to install and then give each commuter catchment exactly one of those installed lockers to use — nothing left unassigned, and no catchment sent to more than one locker. In the end, the tally is the sum of the installation fees for the chosen lockers plus all the detour costs for commuters, and the plan that keeps that total as small as possible is the one they want. Concrete details about candidate locations, fees, and detour distances are shown below.

